Here's a function in JavaScript that checks if any of the numbers in an array of 5 numbers is a valid prime number and prints the number if found:
```javascript
function findPrimeNumber(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    // Check if the current number is greater than 1
    if (arr[i] > 1) {
      let isPrime = true;
      for (let j = 2; j < arr[i]; j++) {
        if (arr[i] % j === 0) {
          // Found a factor, not prime
          isPrime = false;
          break;
        }
      }
      if (isPrime) {
        console.log(`Found a prime number: ${arr[i]}`);
        return;  // Found a prime, no need to continue checking the rest of the array
      }
    }
  }
  console.log("No prime numbers found in the array.");  // If none of the numbers are prime, print this message
}
```
This function iterates through each element in the input array `arr` and checks if it's greater than 1. If it is, it checks if the number is prime by iterating from 2 to the number itself (excluded) and checking for factors. If no factors are found, the number is prime, and it's printed to the console. The function returns after finding a prime number to avoid unnecessary further checks. If none of the numbers are prime, the function prints "No prime numbers found in the array."

